# Tutorial 10 — Choices and Menus
# How to present choices, branch dialogue, and use jump/goto for navigation.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_10_menus
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: You've been clicking through these tutorials using choices this whole time.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Now let's look at how they actually work under the hood — and how to design them well.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to learn about?
> Basic choices -> section_basic
> Branching and merging -> section_branching
> Conditional (gated) choices -> section_gated
> Jump vs goto -> section_jump_goto
> Writing good choice text -> section_writing
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── Basic Choices ───────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_basic
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: A choice is a line starting with {b}>{/b}, followed by the display text and an arrow pointing to a label.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Go to the park -> park_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Stay home -> home_scene{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: When the player reaches these lines, they see buttons. Clicking one jumps to that label.
Lavender: You can have 2, 3, 4 — or even more choices, though 2–4 is the sweet spot for readability.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Every choice needs a matching {b}@label{/b} somewhere in the file, otherwise the engine won't know where to go.
> Next: Branching -> section_branching
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Branching and Merging ───────────────────────────────────────

@label section_branching
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: After a choice, the story branches. Each label contains its own scene, dialogue, or events.
Lavender: The question is: do the branches stay separate, or merge back together?
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}Merge pattern{/b} — both paths eventually [jump] to the same label:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label park_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: What a nice day for a walk.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[jump after_choice]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label home_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: I'll just stay in and read.{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[jump after_choice]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}@label after_choice{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}Lavender: Anyway, where were we?{/color}
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Merging keeps the story manageable — you don't have to maintain two completely separate plotlines.
Lavender: Save permanent splits for major route decisions, not every small choice.
> Next: Gated choices -> section_gated
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Conditional Choices ─────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_gated
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Sometimes a choice should only appear if the player has done something first. That's a {b}gated choice{/b}.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Go to the park -> park_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[if has_key == 1]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Unlock the shed -> shed_scene{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[endif]{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}> Stay home -> home_scene{/color}
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: If the player doesn't have the key, they only see two options. If they do, they see three.
Lavender: Gating creates the illusion of an expanding world — the more the player explores, the more options appear.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Make sure gated content is worth gating. Hidden options should feel rewarding to discover.
> Next: Jump vs goto -> section_jump_goto
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Jump vs Goto ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_jump_goto
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: {b}[jump label]{/b} moves to a label within the {i}same{/i} file. It's the most common navigation command.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[jump after_choice]{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: {b}[goto scenario:label]{/b} moves to a label in a {i}different{/i} scenario file. It crosses file boundaries.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}[goto chapter_2:start]{/color}
Lavender: Use [jump] for in-file navigation — choices, loops, merges. Use [goto] for scene transitions between files.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: A common mistake is using [goto] when [jump] would do. Keep things within one file when possible — it's easier to read and debug.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The tutorial hub uses [goto] to reach each tutorial file. Inside each tutorial, everything is [jump].
> Next: Writing choices -> section_writing
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Writing Good Choice Text ───────────────────────────────────

@label section_writing
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The text on a choice button matters more than you think. A few tips:
narrator: {b}Be specific.{/b} "Go to the park" beats "Option A".
narrator: {b}Hint at consequences.{/b} "Confront him (risky)" tells the player something will happen.
narrator: {b}Match the character's voice.{/b} If the protagonist is sarcastic, the choices should sound sarcastic.
narrator: {b}Keep them short.{/b} One line per choice. If it wraps, it's too long.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: And avoid false choices — two options that lead to the same outcome. Players notice, and it feels cheap.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Every choice should matter, even if only a little. A single [inc] variable makes it feel real.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Choices and navigation cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}> Text -> label{/b} — present a choice that jumps to a label.
narrator: {b}[jump label]{/b} — move to a label in the same file.
narrator: {b}[goto scenario:label]{/b} — move to a label in a different scenario.
narrator: {b}[if condition] > Choice [endif]{/b} — show a choice only when a condition is true.
narrator: {b}Merge pattern:{/b} branch, then [jump] both paths to the same label.
narrator: {b}Tip:{/b} 2–4 choices is the sweet spot. More than 4 overwhelms the player.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Choices are the player's voice in the story. Make every one count.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's build a mini branching scene right now. Ready?
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: It's a rainy afternoon. I'm at the cafe window, watching the street.
Lavender: Someone left an umbrella on the chair next to me.
Lavender: What do you do?
> Take it to the front counter -> practice_counter
> Leave it — not your problem -> practice_leave
> Wait to see if someone comes back -> practice_wait

@label practice_counter
[inc kindness]
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: That's thoughtful. The barista thanks you and puts it behind the counter.
narrator: The rain picks up outside. You feel good about the small gesture.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_leave
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Fair enough. You've got your own things to worry about.
narrator: The umbrella sits there as the cafe fills up. Eventually someone takes it.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wait
[inc kindness]
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Ten minutes pass. Then a woman rushes in, soaked, and spots the umbrella.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: "Oh thank goodness!" She gives you a grateful smile.
narrator: Sometimes patience is its own reward.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: The counter path is short and sweet — action, acknowledgment, done. Quick branches work great for small moments.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: The "do nothing" path is underrated. It's a valid choice that reveals character. Don't forget to write it.
[else]
Lavender: The waiting path is longer but more rewarding. It shows that pacing choices differently creates texture.
[endif]
Lavender: Notice how all three paths merge here. Same scene continues, different flavor. That's the merge pattern in action.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_2]
